This has been discussed with @lnkuiper in the context of improving this pattern:
```c++
	TaskExecutor executor(scheduler, TaskSchedulerType::ASYNC);

	for (idx_t idx = 0; idx < num_blocks; idx++) {
		executor.ScheduleTask(make_uniq<FetchBlockTask>(*this, executor, context,
		                                                external_file_cache.GetBufferManager(), blocks[idx],
		                                                first_block + idx, block_size, pins[idx]));
	}
	executor.WorkOnTasks();
```
Here tasks will be handed out, most likely to IO-threads, while this thread (likely a regular one) will stay idle waiting for the others to come back.
A minor optimization, that is though easy to roll, is: let's park the first produced task, and have WorkOnTask first perform the parked task, and only then wait on the others (possibly picking them up more in the process).

This is in most cases invisible, since in most cases bottleneck is either compute or network, and this matters only in balanced cases, say for example you have 4 compute threads + 40 IO ones. And say that by chance you got a single fetch task. Before, you would block 1 compute thread + 1 IO one, with the first just idle waiting for the second. After the PR, compute thread will do the fetch itself, freeing 1 IO thread.

This is relevant only in minor cases, but it was still a smell and flagged as improvements.

PR here adds an optional `TaskExecutorMode`, by default `TaskExecutorMode::BACKGROUND`, but one can opt in to `TaskExecutorMode::JOINED` where first produced task is parked and done in the same thread doing the scheduling.
